> The error you're encountering suggests that the `loadDelimited` function is returning a pointer of type `GeographicMap<double>*`, but you're trying to store this in a variable of type `MatrixGeographicMap<double>*`. The same issue occurs with `GeographicMap<bool>*`. The compiler cannot automatically convert these pointer types because `MatrixGeographicMap` might not be derived from `GeographicMap`, or even if it is, it might not have a direct pointer conversion.
> 
> If you know for certain that the object returned by `loadDelimited` is indeed a `MatrixGeographicMap`, you can use a `static_cast` to perform the conversion. However, you should be very sure about this, as incorrect casting can lead to undefined behavior. Here's how you might modify your code:
> 
> ```cpp
> auto* geographic_map_coarse = openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<double>::loadDelimited(latitudes, longitudes, "mask_new.tsv", NULL, '\t');
> mask_coarse = static_cast<openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<double>*>(geographic_map_coarse);
> 
> auto* geographic_map_fine = openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<bool>::loadDelimited(
>     openworld::DividedRange::withEnds(29.74583, 33.9125, 0.008333334, openworld::Inds::lat),
>     openworld::DividedRange::withEnds(74.77084, 85.17084, 0.008333334, openworld::Inds::lon),
>     "mask.tsv", NULL, '\t');
> mask_fine = static_cast<openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<bool>*>(geographic_map_fine);
> ```
> 
> Make sure to validate that `loadDelimited` correctly returns a pointer to a `MatrixGeographicMap`, perhaps by examining the documentation or implementation of the library you're working with.
> 
> If you do not have access to that, and `static_cast` does not suffice (perhaps due to inheritance issues), consider using `dynamic_cast` if `MatrixGeographicMap` is polymorphic and you want to ensure type safety:
> 
> ```cpp
> mask_coarse = dynamic_cast<openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<double>*>(geographic_map_coarse);
> mask_fine = dynamic_cast<openworld::MatrixGeographicMap<bool>*>(geographic_map_fine);
> ```
> 
> With `dynamic_cast`, if the cast fails, it will set the pointer to `nullptr`. It can be a safer option as it allows you to check the validity of the cast at runtime. However, it incurs runtime overhead and requires `GeographicMap` to have at least one virtual method for polymorphism support.
> 
> Always handle potential failures of `dynamic_cast` by checking if the resultant pointer is `nullptr` before using it.
